      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Transitive verbs require a direct object to complete their meaning, whereas Intransitive verbs do not.",
        "Finite verbs change their form according to the tense of the sentence and the person/number of the subject.",
        "Non-Finite verbs (Infinitives, Gerunds, Participles) do not change form based on subject or tense.",
        "Gerunds function as verbal nouns, while Participles function as verbal adjectives.",
        "Causative verbs (e.g., make, get, have, let) express an action that the subject causes another agent to perform."
      ],
      "id": "ub78b56"
    },
